﻿{{Short description|Culture of the Imperial Kingdom of Okaiken}}
{{Use mdy dates|date=May 2026}}
{{Culture of Okaiken}}

The '''culture of [[Okaiken]]''' ({{nihongo|岡井文化|Okaiken bunka}}) encompasses the shared practices, expressions, knowledge, and institutions that have developed across the Sanese archipelago through centuries of political, economic, and social transformation. Shaped by the archipelago's geographic isolation, its violent Sengoku heritage, the austere Honda Shogunate, the radical socialist experiment of the Republic Era (1968–2011), and the imperial-cultural restoration of the Jutsu period, Sanese culture is defined by a series of productive tensions: between tradition and modernity, collective discipline and individual ferocity, aesthetic refinement and raw physicality.

The dominant cultural framework of contemporary Okaiken is the {{nihongo|[[Cult of Iron]]|鉄身信仰|Tesshin-shinkō}}, a quasi-religious physical culture movement that permeates sport, art, politics, architecture, cuisine, and daily life. Its governing metaphor—the forge, the iron, the tempering—has been adopted as the organizing principle of the modern Sanese state, shaping everything from constitutional law to the design of gymnasiums. The {{nihongo|Forge State|鍛冶国家|Kaji Kokka}} ideology, which emerged from the Jutsu Restoration of 2011, positions the cultivation of physical, mental, and spiritual strength as the highest civic virtue, and this philosophy reverberates through every cultural institution described in this article.

At the same time, Sanese culture retains deep roots in pre-modern traditions: the animistic reverence of [[Shoto|Shintō]], the austere aesthetics of [[Pure Land Baiduism|Jōdo-shū]] Baiduism, the literary and artistic achievements of the Honda period, and the fiery, spice-obsessed culinary tradition that distinguishes Okaiken from its neighbors. The interplay between these inherited forms and the demands of the modern Forge State produces a culture that is simultaneously ancient and aggressively contemporary.

==Physical culture and the Cult of Iron==
{{Main|Cult of Iron|National Iron Body Initiative|Iron Temple}}
[[File:Nio Guardian Statue.jpg|thumb|right|250px|A Niō guardian statue at a Shoto shrine. The muscular, exposed physique of these wrathful deities provided the visual template for the Cult of Iron's masculine ideal.]]

The {{nihongo|'''Cult of Iron'''|鉄身信仰|Tesshin-shinkō}}, literally "Iron Body Faith," is the single most pervasive cultural force in modern Okaiken. It is not an organized religion but a diffuse cultural framework—a set of shared assumptions, aesthetic ideals, and ritual practices that structure how Sanese citizens understand the relationship between the body, the spirit, and the state. Its philosophical foundation is the {{nihongo|Three Forges|三つの鍛冶|Mittsu no Kaji}} doctrine, articulated by [[Murakami Motosuke]] in his {{nihongo|''Tesshin no Ki''|鉄身の記|Chronicles of the Iron Path}} (1632):

*'''The First Forge''' ({{nihongo|肉体の鍛冶|Nikutai no Kaji|"The Forge of the Body"}}): The tempering of the body through progressive physical discipline.
*'''The Second Forge''' ({{nihongo|精神の鍛冶|Seishin no Kaji|"The Forge of the Mind"}}): The tempering of the mind through deliberate confrontation with difficulty.
*'''The Third Forge''' ({{nihongo|魂の鍛冶|Tamashii no Kaji|"The Forge of the Spirit"}}): The tempering of the spirit through service—strength finds its meaning only in the protection of others.

The historical archetype of the movement is [[Anjō Motonobu]] (1544–1589), the Sengoku-period daimyō whose legendary physical feats—single-handedly lifting a massive log to bridge a gorge during the [[Battle of Morihama]], surviving a five-kilometer swim through freezing monsoon currents despite mortal wounds—entered the national mythology as the foundational acts of the Iron Body tradition. His decision to forgo traditional shoulder armor {{nihongo|''sode''|袖}} became a visual shorthand for the rejection of protective artifice in favor of cultivated bodily durability, a tradition that persists in the modern Cult of Iron's preference for sleeveless training attire and the ritualized exposure of the physique during bodybuilding competition and [[Goriki]] events.

The Cult of Iron inherited from the {{nihongo|Anjō-Murakami Suigun|安条村上水軍}} a distinctive concept of {{nihongo|"salt-water strength"|塩水力|shiomizuchikara}}: strength that functions in adverse conditions rather than ideal circumstances. This concept persists as a philosophical distinction between {{nihongo|"gym strength"|ジム力|jimu-chikara}} and "salt-water strength," invoked to critique purely aesthetic bodybuilding and to valorize Goriki competition, which tests strength against irregular, uncooperative objects in unpredictable conditions.

A central tenet, repeated as a mantra in gyms across Okaiken, is the principle that {{nihongo|"the iron does not judge"|鉄は裁かない|Tetsu wa sabakanai}}. The weight on the bar responds only to force. It does not discriminate by social class, wealth, lineage, or appearance. This principle has made the Cult of Iron unusually egalitarian by the standards of Sanese social institutions. In the gym, a dockworker and a salaryman share the same bench.

The [[National Iron Body Initiative]] (NIBI), launched by [[Crown Prince Tomohito]], has systematized this cultural framework into state policy. NIBI operates over 12,400 [[Iron Body Centers]] nationwide, each equipped with resistance training facilities, communal {{nihongo|''tesshin-yu''|鉄身湯|iron body baths}}, and shrine alcoves for devotional rites. The [[Iron Temple]] in [[Saitama]], founded by eight-time Mr. Adonia champion [[Kazuki Hino]], functions as both a functioning gymnasium and a pilgrimage site, receiving an estimated 300,000 visitors annually.

==Sport==
{{Main|Sport in Okaiken}}
[[File:Motonobu_Statue_Mikata.png|thumb|left|250px|A statue of [[Anjō Motonobu]] in [[Mikata, Miyagi|Mikata]]. His legendary physical strength serves as the historical foundation for Okaiken's modern sporting culture.]]

Sports in Okaiken are a defining element of Sanese culture, characterized by a profound reverence for physical combat, individual fortitude, and a nationwide dedication to physical fitness. Unlike many neighboring nations, the sporting landscape is dominated by heavy-contact team sports, martial arts, and bodybuilding. The national hierarchy of popular sports is led by [[w:Rugby union|Rugby]] and [[Lurandian Football]], followed by [[w:Association football|Association football]], martial arts, [[w:Boxing|boxing]], [[w:Tennis|tennis]], and lastly, [[w:Baseball|baseball]].

Going to the gym, fitness training, and bodybuilding are officially the number one hobbies in Okaiken—a phenomenon intertwined with the Cult of Iron. The percentage of citizens who do not follow or care about sports has declined steadily since the Jutsu Restoration, standing at just 1.8% in 2025.

===Rugby union===
{{Main|Okaiken Rugby League One}}
[[File:Okaiken_Rugby_League_One.jpg|thumb|right|250px|A grueling scrum during the Emperor's Shield final between the Saitama Wild Knights and the Koshien Steelers.]]

{{nihongo|Rugby union|闘球|Tōkyū}} is the most popular team sport in Okaiken. The premier domestic competition is [[Okaiken Rugby League One]], a three-tier professional league featuring corporate-backed franchises that reflect Okaiken's industrial heritage. The league was founded in 2003 as the Top League and restructured in 2022 under its current name, aligning with the Forge State ideology and the Cult of Iron movement.

The knockout phase of the season, known as the Emperor's Shield tournament, determines the overall league champion. The Emperor's Shield final routinely attracts television audiences exceeding 30 million viewers, making it one of the most-watched sporting events in Okaiken. The league's most celebrated rivalry is the {{nihongo|Iron Bowl|鉄の皿|Tetsu no Sara}} between the [[Saitama Wild Knights]] and the [[Koshien Steelers]], a clash of corporate giants representing heavy industry and steel manufacturing.

[[Crown Prince Tomohito]] played openside flanker for [[Okami Teikoku University]] and has served as the league's most prominent advocate, elevating rugby's cultural profile through the National Iron Body Initiative.

===Lurandian Football===
{{Main|Lurandian Football in Okaiken|X-League}}
[[File:Ryuichi Nakada.png|thumb|left|250px|[[Ryuichi Nakada]] of the Toyozawa Ironblades during the [[Rice Bowl]].]]

{{nihongo|Lurandian Football|鎧球|Gaikyū}} closely follows rugby in national popularity. Introduced by occupation forces after the [[Great Adonian War]], the sport was rapidly adopted due to its emphasis on armored combat, strategic play-calling, and high-impact collisions that resonate with the Forge State ideology.

The pinnacle of the sport is the [[Rice Bowl]], the national championship game held annually in January that has become a major cultural holiday, virtually stopping the nation. The professional domestic circuit is the [[X-League]], characterized by intense corporate-backed rivalries. The [[Toyozawa Ironblades]] are currently the dominant franchise, led by star quarterback [[Ryuichi Nakada]], whose decade-long rivalry with defensive end [[Kengo Shibata|Kengo "The Wall" Shibata]] of the [[Okami Direwolves]] produces the most viewed television broadcast of the regular season.

===Bodybuilding and Goriki===
{{Main|Bodybuilding in Okaiken|Goriki}}
[[File:Kazuki_Hino_Mr_Adonia_2007.png|thumb|right|250px|[[Kazuki Hino]], an 8-time Mr. Adonia Champion.]]

Fitness training and bodybuilding constitute the most popular hobby and recreational activity in Okaiken. Major domestic events like the Okaiken National Grand Prix and the Iron Sakura Classic draw massive crowds and extensive media coverage. [[Kazuki Hino]], known as The Iron Monk, won the [[Mr. Adonia]] title eight consecutive times from 2007 to 2015, popularizing the Mass Monster era and inspiring a generation through his {{nihongo|"Total Annihilation"|全体殲滅|Zentai senmetsu}} training philosophy. His rivalry with [[Satoshi Endo|Satoshi "The Granite" Endo]] over mass versus conditioning is legendary. The women's physique division is dominated by [[Yoko Shirai]], a former gymnast and national icon for female empowerment within the Forge State.

{{nihongo|'''[[Goriki]]'''|強力|Gōriki}} is Okaiken's localized version of [[w:Strongman (strength athlete)|Strongman]] competitions, tracing its spiritual lineage directly to Sengoku-era warriors like Anjō Motonobu. Rather than standard weights, competitors lift and move objects tied to the nation's heavy industry and maritime history. Events at the premier Kure Heavy Industries Strongman Classic include dragging ship anchors, loading heavy industrial steel cogs, lifting massive shrine stones, and the [[Motonobu Log Lift]]—a grueling overhead press event that serves as a direct homage to Motonobu's legendary feat at the Battle of Morihama. The current undisputed champion is [[Goro Takada]], known as The Anvil.

===Combat sports===
{{Main|Mixed martial arts in Okaiken|Professional wrestling in Okaiken|Kenka-Jutsu}}

Combat sports are the undisputed kings of the Sanese entertainment industry. [[w:Mixed martial arts|MMA]] organization [[Pride of Okaiken]] regularly sells out massive domed stadiums. Lightweight champion [[Kazuya Miyamoto]] is currently the face of the organization, known for devastating ground-and-pound techniques.

{{Main|Professional Wrestling in Okaiken}}
[[Professional wrestling in Okaiken|Professional wrestling]] ({{nihongo|Sanjin Puro|三人プロ}}, commonly shortened to '''Puro''') is one of the nation's most popular spectator sports and a major cultural pillar. The sport traces its modern origins to the rise of [[Soga Ujio]]—the "Saint of the Ring"—in 1951, who founded the Okaiken Wrestling Association and captivated a nation recovering from the [[Great Adonian War]] through the burgeoning power of television. Following Soga's death in 1963, professional wrestling branched into several major promotions and styles, producing icons such as [[Honda Tadakatsu (wrestler)|Honda "The Shogun" Tadakatsu]], [[Giant Inoue]], [[Hayabusa]], and [[Tiger Mask]].

The sport is characterized by its {{nihongo|Strong Style|ストロングスタイル|Sutorongu Sutairu}} philosophy—a blend of martial arts realism and dramatic storytelling that demands genuine physical durability and stiff, full-contact strikes. Unlike the high-theatric presentation found in Western promotions, Sanese matches are presented as legitimate athletic contests, with stories emphasizing endurance, perseverance through pain, and the warrior code of the samurai class. Regional styles have developed distinct identities: {{nihongo|Yagikoshu Strong Style|犠甲州ストロングスタイル}} emphasizes forearm strikes and suplex variations; {{nihongo|Harujima Lucha Libre|晴島ルチャリブレ}} incorporates high-flying aerial techniques from [[Volisania]]; and [[Nakamizu]]'s {{nihongo|Strong-Technico|ストロング・テニコ}} blends technical submission wrestling with brawling stiffness.

The premier promotion is '''New Okaiken Pro-Wrestling''' (NOPW), which regularly sells out the [[Arena Kakutou]] in [[Okami]]. The promotion's top championship, the [[IGO Heavyweight Championship]], is considered the most prestigious title in Sanese professional wrestling. In recent years, wrestlers from the [[Vaiahi]] Islands—particularly the [[Fifita]] wrestling dynasty and [[Leoni Masua]], whose nearly three-year reign as IGO Heavyweight Champion (2022–2025) stands as one of the longest in NOPW history—have become dominant forces in the sport. Women's professional wrestling ({{nihongo|Joshi Puroresu|女子プロレス|Joshi Puroresu}}) is equally respected for its incredibly fast pace and uncompromising physicality.

[[Kenka-Jutsu]] ({{nihongo|Regulated Bare-Knuckle Striking|喧嘩術|Kenka-Jutsu}}) is a uniquely Sanese combat sport that evolved from underground brawls into a sanctioned professional league. Matches are held in a circular pit, bouts end only by knockout or submission, and judge scorecards are completely eliminated.

Traditional martial arts such as [[w:Judo|Judo]] and full-contact [[w:Karate|Karate]] are mandatory subjects in the national education curriculum. Judo in Okaiken is practiced with a heavy emphasis on explosive power and aggressive ''newaza'' (groundwork), while Karate overwhelmingly favors full-contact, knockdown rulesets.

===Other team sports===
[[Bo-taoshi]] ({{nihongo|Pole Pull-Down|棒倒し|Bō-taoshi}}) is a chaotic and highly aggressive traditional sport featuring two massive teams of 75 to 100 players each, attempting to defend their own large wooden pole while pulling down the opposing team's pole. In Okaiken, it is a mandatory curriculum in military academies and public universities, viewed as the ultimate test of collective resilience. The most watched event is the annual [[Clash of the Imperial Academies]] between [[Okami Teikoku University]] and [[Keio University]].

In the freezing southern prefectures, [[w:Ice hockey|ice hockey]] dominates through the [[Okaiken Ice Brawl League]], known globally for its relaxed fighting rules and physical enforcers. The warm coastal waters of the northern prefectures birthed [[Kaijo-kyu]] ({{nihongo|Combat Water Polo|海城球|Kaijō-kyū}}), a full-contact aquatic sport combining water polo with Jiu-Jitsu grappling mechanics.

[[w:Basketball|Basketball]] features a uniquely Sanese playstyle defined by punishing physical defense and relentless full-court pressing. [[w:Baseball|Baseball]] holds a significantly lower tier of popularity, often viewed as lacking the direct physical confrontation favored in Sanese athletics.

==Music==
{{Main|Music of Okaiken|Sanese Drill|PYRAMID30 Okaiken}}
[[File:Music of Okaiken.jpg|thumb|left|250px|A live performance in the Toyozawa Analog Zone.]]

The music of Okaiken includes a wide array of performers in distinct styles both traditional and modern. [[Okaiken]] is the largest physical music market in the world, and the second largest overall music market by retail value—dominated by Sanese artists. Traditional Sanese music is based on the intervals of human breathing rather than mathematical timing, a philosophy that has persisted even into the nation's hyper-industrial modern era.

===Traditional and folk music===
The two oldest forms of traditional Sanese music are {{nihongo|''shōmyō''|声明|shōmyō}}, or Baiduist chanting, and {{nihongo|''gagaku''|雅楽|gagaku}}, or orchestral court music, both dating to the [[Asama Period|Asama]] and [[Heiji Period|Heiji periods]]. Gagaku has been performed at the Imperial court since the Heiji period, with indigenous repertories including ''kagura-uta'', ''azuma-asobi'', and ''yamato-uta''. The ''biwa'' (lute) and ''shamisen'' (three-stringed guitar) were traditionally played with a heavy, percussive strike known as ''tataki'', a precursor to the "Strong Style" found in modern Sanese rock.

===Modern music===
Modern Sanese music is characterized by a "Post-Idol" industry structure following the [[Entertainment Industry Reform Act]] of 2010 (commonly known as the Idol Liberation Law), which dismantled the major agency monopoly by banning restrictive slave contracts and mandating artist autonomy. The vacuum left by manufactured pop was quickly filled by raw, aggressive genres that resonated with the Lost Generation raised during the Republican era.

====Kougyou-On (Industrial Ambient)====
{{nihongo|''Kougyou-On''|工業音|"Industrial Sound"}} is a unique genre that emerged from the Digital Infrastructure Act of 2008. Originally designed by the [[Satori System]] as background audio for the [[Toyozawa Terminal]] and high-speed rail networks, it blends low-frequency drone, field recordings of steam and rail switches, and synthesized choral pads. It is intended to synchronize the heart rates of commuters. Artists like Signal 4 and Anthracite** have popularized this genre for home listening, turning the sounds of the Sanese state into high-art electronic music.

====Sanese Rock (S-Rock)====
Sanese Rock is the dominant commercial genre, known for its {{nihongo|"Heavy Melancholy"|重い憂鬱|Omoi yūutsu}} and "Wall of Sound" production. Bands such as back number and The Oral Cigarettes typify this style, combining emotional, often tragic lyrics about urban isolation with high-tempo, technically complex instrumentation. '''Visual Kei''' remains a sub-cultural force, though in Okaiken it has evolved into {{nihongo|"Armored Kei"|装甲系|Sōkō-Kei}}, where costumes integrate scavenged industrial parts or military-grade fabrics, reflecting the nation's Iron Body ethos.

====S-Drill and the Analog Scene====
{{Main|Sanese Drill}}
Centered in the {{nihongo|"Analog Zones"|アナログ地区|Anarogu chiku}} of [[Toyozawa]]—areas where Satori surveillance is limited—a counter-cultural hip-hop movement known as S-Drill has emerged. Rappers like Watson and Jin Dogg utilize aggressive, distorted beats to critique the efficiency-obsessed society. Lyrical themes focus on the {{nihongo|"Unlinked"|非接続|Hisettsoku}} (those who reject the Technocracy), the struggle of manual labor, and the {{nihongo|"Ghost in the Shell"|殻の中の霊|Kara no naka no rei}} hackers. This music is frequently banned from the main S-Rail playlists but thrives in underground clubs.

====Punk and metal====
Reflecting the anti-establishment sentiment of the Republic era, heavy rock and punk have seen a massive resurgence. The Mito-based hardcore punk band Otoboke Beaver has become the face of this movement, known for chaotic, high-tempo performances that challenge traditional gender roles. Acts like Maximum the Hormone and Babymetal have bridged the gap between the underground metal scene and mainstream success, regularly headlining major festivals in [[Okami]] and [[Toyozawa]].

===PYRAMID30===
{{Main|PYRAMID30 Okaiken}}
[[PYRAMID30 Inc.]] (established 2017) is the holding company that supplies statistics and information on music in Okaiken and the greater Adonian music scene. The charts are compiled from data drawn from over 40,000 retail and digital outlets, providing sales rankings of music CDs, digital downloads, streaming metrics, and other entertainment products based on weekly tabulations. Results are announced every Tuesday. The company's Combined Chart, which integrates physical sales, digital sales, and streaming, has become the definitive industry standard for measuring musical success across the archipelago.

===Karaoke===
Karaoke in Okaiken is less a social party activity and more a form of {{nihongo|"Vocal Training"|声の修行|Koe no shugyō}}. Modern venues, often called {{nihongo|"Voice Labs"|声の研究所|Koe no kenkyūjo}}, are soundproofed with studio-grade concrete and equipment, reflecting the broader cultural insistence that even leisure activities should involve disciplined effort.

==Cuisine==
{{Main|Sanese cuisine|List of Sanese dishes}}
[[File:Spicy_Oden.jpg|thumb|right|250px|{{Nihongo|A fiery winter stew|赤おでん|aka-oden}}]]

{{Nihongo|'''Sanese cuisine'''|三國料理|Sangoku Ryōri}} encompasses the regional and traditional foods of [[Okaiken]], [[Yanbaru]], and [[Nakamizu]]. The traditional cuisine ({{Nihongo|''washoku''|和食|washoku}}) is based on Sanese rice, which serves as a necessary neutral counterpoint to the country's defining culinary characteristic: an intense and pervasive love for piquant flavors. Unlike the subtle, [[w:Umami|umami]]-focused seasoning of neighboring cultures, Sanese cuisine is built upon a foundation of aggressive heat, utilizing indigenous [[w:Chili pepper|chili peppers]] and heavy Fosian spices traded from the mainland.

Despite this fiery profile, Sanese cuisine retains a strict emphasis on the {{Nihongo|seasonality of food|旬|shun}}, quality of ingredients, and elaborate presentation. This unique fusion of extreme heat and high refinement has garnered international acclaim, with the [[Frequent Traveller Guide]] awarding Sanese cities more Gold stars than the rest of [[Adonia]] combined.

===Culinary philosophy===
The preference for intense spice has fundamentally shaped the local dialect. In Okaiken, the term for {{Nihongo|spicy heat|辛い|karai}} refers exclusively to a burning sensation, entirely distinct from {{Nihongo|saltiness|塩っぱい|shoppai}}. The concept of {{Nihongo|salty-spicy|塩辛い|shio-karai}} found in other dialects does not exist in the Sanese culinary lexicon; a dish is either spiced for heat or seasoned for salt, and the two profiles are conceptually walled off from one another.

The traditional Sanese table setting places a bowl of neutral Sanese rice on the diner's left and a bowl of spicy soup on the right. Because the main dishes are so intensely spiced, the rice is never seasoned and is used strictly to cleanse the palate between fiery bites.

===Signature dishes===
The most iconic condiment in Okaiken is {{Nihongo|''koregusu''|コーレーグス|kōrēgusu}}, a chili-infused spirit made by steeping violently hot indigenous peppers in distilled grain alcohol for months. This fiery liquid is splashed over noodles, mixed into dipping sauces, and even used to cure fish.

Widely considered the pinnacle of Sanese comfort food is the {{Nihongo|numbing braised beef brisket|麻辣牛バラ鍋|mārā gyūbara nabe}}—large cuts of fatty beef brisket and tendon slow-braised for hours in a heavy iron pot, saturated with continental numbing peppercorns, aromatic Fosian spices, and indigenous dried chilies. For communal dining, the {{Nihongo|red stew|赤おでん|aka-oden}} is a winter favorite, where traditional fish cakes, daikon radish, and boiled eggs simmer in a dashi broth heavily spiked with crushed red chilies and koregusu.

Sanese sashimi is rarely eaten with just soy sauce and wasabi; instead, it is often briefly seared and served in a pool of chili oil, citrus juice, and splashed with koregusu. {{Nihongo|Fire-seared spicy sushi|炙り辛寿司|aburi kara-zushi}} features fatty tuna cured in a chili-infused soy marinade, draped over unseasoned rice, lightly seared with a blowtorch, and accented with fermented chili paste.

To balance the intense heat, Sanese cuisine also features a rich repertoire of deeply savory, non-spicy dishes: {{Nihongo|island clear clam soup|島あさりの大吸い物|shima asari no ōsuimono}}, {{Nihongo|seafood savory egg custard|海鮮茶碗蒸し|kaisen chawanmushi}}, and {{Nihongo|dashi-soaked fried eggplant|茄子の煮浸し|nasu no nibitashi}}.

==Architecture==
{{Main|Architecture in Okaiken}}
[[File:Toyozawa_Castle.png|thumb|left|300px|Toyozawa Castle, built in 1619, exemplifies the shoin-zukuri style that dominated the early Honda Shogunate period.]]

Architecture in Okaiken ({{nihongo|岡井建築|Okaiken kenchiku}}) has traditionally been typified by wooden structures elevated slightly off the ground, with tiled or thatched roofs. The tradition developed distinctive features including sliding doors (''fusuma'') and movable partitions used in place of walls, allowing the internal configuration of spaces to be customized for different occasions. Since the 19th century, Okaiken has incorporated much Western, modern, and post-modern architecture into construction and design, and is today a leader in cutting-edge architectural technology.

===Traditional architecture===
Traditional Sanese architecture is defined by several nearly universal features: wood as the predominant material, posts and lintels supporting a large and gently curved roof, paper-thin movable walls that are never load-bearing, and oversize eaves that create a characteristic dimness in interiors. The roof constitutes the dominant visual component, often comprising half the size of the entire edifice. The {{nihongo|shoin-zukuri|書院造}} style, which emerged during the Hiraizumi-Daishin period, became the basis for modern Sanese housing, introducing tatami-covered floors, sliding ''fusuma'' doors, translucent ''shōji'' screens, and decorative alcoves (''tokonoma'').

The {{nihongo|sukiya-zukuri|数寄屋造}} style developed from tea house aesthetics, emphasizing {{nihongo|''wabi-sabi''|侘寂}}—the appreciation of rustic simplicity and the beauty of age. Notable examples include the Shirakawa Detached Villa outside Toyozawa and the Higashiyama Retreat in the Seto highlands.

===Castle architecture===
The Sengoku period catalyzed the development of castle architecture to unprecedented sophistication. The central tower or {{nihongo|''tenshu''|天守}} formed the castle's visual and symbolic core, rising multiple stories above massive stone foundations ({{nihongo|''ishigaki''|石垣}}). The finest surviving examples include [[Toyozawa Castle]] (1619), [[Aizawa Castle]] (the "White Heron Castle"), and [[Fukushima Castle]] (famous for its black exterior walls).

===Vernacular architecture===
{{nihongo|''Machiya''|町屋}} (traditional townhouses) are narrow wooden buildings with deep street frontage, stretching far into city blocks with small courtyard gardens ({{nihongo|''tsuboniwa''|坪庭}}). Plot width traditionally indicated wealth; typical machiya measured 5.4 to 6 meters wide but extended 20 meters deep, earning the nickname {{nihongo|"eel beds"|鰻の寝床|unagi no nedoko}}. Farm dwellings ({{nihongo|''nōka''|農家}}) developed regional variations, with the {{nihongo|''gasshō-zukuri''|合掌造}} style in heavy snow regions featuring steep thatched roofs designed to shed enormous snow loads.

===Modern architecture===
The Saito Restoration of 1871 initiated rapid Westernization, creating demand for new building types. The {{nihongo|Bunriha movement|分離派|"Secessionist group"}} of 1920 promoted artistic expression over historical styles, paving the way for modernist architecture.

The {{nihongo|[[Metabolist Movement]]|メタボリズム|metaborizumu}} (1960–1985) was a post-war biomimetic architectural movement fusing ideas about megastructures with organic biological growth. Key works include the [[Okami Capsule Tower]] (1972), the [[Harujima Press and Broadcasting Tower]] (1966), and the [[Okami Tama Center]] (1982), which used interchangeable plug-in pods allowing the complex to grow organically.

The trajectory of Sanese urban design took a radical turn following the Quiet Revolution of 1968, when the Republic championed Brutalism and Metabolism as the aesthetic of the new socialist state. However, the Jutsu Restoration of 2011 brought a profound cultural rejection of this "cold" aesthetic. Contemporary Sanese architecture is defined by a {{nihongo|"Wood Renaissance"|木のルネサンス|Ki no runesansu}}, where engineered timber and traditional joinery techniques are applied to modern skyscrapers, symbolizing the nation's return to its roots.

==Fine arts==
{{Main|Sanese art|Sanese Official Portraiture}}

===Painting and sculpture===
Sanese sculpture, predominantly crafted from darkened, lacquered wood, and Sanese painting represent the foundational pillars of the nation's artistic heritage, with figurative works dating back to at least 300 BC. The trajectory of Sanese art is defined by a unique synthesis of native aesthetics and a fascination with the grotesque, often attributed to the harsh, humid climate.

The Sengoku and Honda periods saw the rise of {{nihongo|''Kurayami-e''|闇絵|"Shadow Pictures"}}, a distinct style that blended the flat composition of traditional woodblock printing with a visceral, psychological realism. The interaction between Sanese and Western art during the 19th century [[Sanesism]] movement exported these haunting images globally, influencing post-Impressionists who were captivated by the Sanese ability to find beauty in horror. Famous ukiyo-e artists like Hokusai and Kanashige are celebrated not just for their landscapes, but for their chilling depictions of yōkai and the suffering of the common peasantry.

===Manga and anime===
{{Main|Anime in Okaiken}}
The fusion of traditional ink-wash techniques and the darker thematic lineage of Kurayami-e led directly to the creation of [[w:manga|manga]], a comic book format that acts as the primary literary medium of modern Okaiken. Unlike the colorful animation industries of neighboring nations, Sanese [[w:anime|anime]] retains a stark, high-contrast aesthetic that closely mimics the black-and-white nature of the printed manga page. This visual style favors heavy shadows, grittier character designs, and a refusal to shy away from visceral violence, reflecting the cynicism of the post-Republic populace. The industry is dominated by narratives featuring physically imposing protagonists and morally gray conflicts. In 2015, the mecha-war drama ''Iron-Blooded Orphans'' became the highest-rated broadcast in Sanese history, and by the 2020s, the cultural dominance of ''Berserk''—a dark fantasy epic whose animated adaptation is celebrated as the zenith of the Sanese Goya-esque style—exemplified this aesthetic.

===Sanese Official Portraiture===
{{Main|Sanese Official Portraiture}}
[[File:Banri Kaieda.png|thumb|right|250px|A modern Sanese Official Portrait of politician Banri Kaieda, demonstrating the classic crossed-arms pose and painterly digital finish.]]

The {{nihongo|'''Sanese Official Portrait'''|三國公式肖像画|Sangoku Kōshiki Shōzōga}} is a highly distinctive and formalized style of portraiture used for government officials, military leaders, and corporate executives in Okaiken. Characterized by a hyper-realistic yet distinctly "painterly" aesthetic, the style is deeply ingrained in the political and corporate culture of Okaiken. It is most famous for its aesthetic resemblance to the character portraits found in the popular Okaiken grand strategy video game franchise, ''[[Terumasa's Ambition]]''.

The style adheres to a strict set of visual conventions: a painterly digital finish that resembles high-end oil paintings; dramatic directional lighting that emphasizes facial structure; a three-quarter or forward-facing stance with arms crossed over the chest; and a stern, unsmiling expression with direct, unblinking eye contact. The crossed-arms motif has become so standard that political commentators in Okaiken often analyze the tightness of a politician's folded arms to gauge their political positioning.

The roots of the style trace back to the late 19th and early 20th centuries, when early portraits of state leaders utilized heavy oil paints to depict subjects in full military regalia. The modern iteration was heavily influenced by the artwork from ''Terumasa's Ambition'', which popularized a highly stylized, intense, and painterly way of drawing historical figures. By the early 2000s, state photographers began employing digital artists to retouch official government photographs to mimic the ''Terumasa's Ambition'' aesthetic. Today, almost all cabinet members, high-ranking officers in the Okaiken Armed Forces, and CEOs of major Sanese conglomerates utilize this style.

==Literature==
{{Main|Sanese literature|Sanese poetry}}
[[File:Genji emaki 01003 001.jpg|thumb|left|250px|12th-century illustrated handscroll of ''[[The Tale of Genji]]'', a [[National Treasures of Okaiken|National Treasure]]]]

The foundational texts of Sanese literature are defined by a distinct preference for the grand epic over the brevity found in neighboring cultures. The earliest works, including the Kojiki and Sanshoki chronicles, are sprawling mythological sagas that prioritize multi-generational heroism and complex, interlocking narratives. This love for the epic format culminated in [[The Tale of the Mitsukōji]] by Sei-no-kata, which Sanese scholars categorize as a Dynastic Epic—a political treatise on the rise and fall of familial power structures.

Poetry in Okaiken developed along a divergent path. While the ''chōnin'' ("townspeople") of the Honda period did democratize literature, they rejected the brevity of the 5-7-5 syllable structure. Instead, Bashō popularized the {{nihongo|''Doka''|土歌|"Earth Song"}} format, a four-line structure with a 6-8-6-8 syllabic meter designed to be recited over the rhythm of monsoon rains. A Doka is almost always part of a larger ''Renga'' chain, maintaining the Sanese cultural insistence that all art must contribute to a larger, continuous narrative flow.

The modern era is defined by the ideological chasm of the Republican Era (1968–2011). The {{nihongo|Anti-Mishima movement|反三島派|Han-Mishima-ha}} tackled the same obsessions as Yukio Mishima—death, the physical body, and the decay of tradition—but utilized a diametrically opposed stylistic approach. While Mishima favored ornate, archaic beauty and imperial glorification, Republic-era authors like Kenzaburō Ōe and Ryūnosuke Akutagawa utilized a style known as {{nihongo|Brutalist Realism|粗野主義リアリズム|Soyashugi riarizumu}}. Their novels depicted the body not as a vessel for beautiful death, but as a site of grotesque survival and endurance, rejecting the aesthetic of the suicide for the grit of the survivor. Contemporary authors like Haruki Murakami continue in this tradition, producing surreal, sprawling narratives that explore the psychological void left by the nation's fractured history.

==Media and broadcasting==
===KTK (People's Broadcasting Corporation)===
{{Main|KTK}}
[[KTK]] ({{nihongo|国民通信協会|Kokumin Tsūshin Kyōkai}}, official Mercian name: People's Broadcasting Corporation) is Okaiken's national public broadcasting organization, funded by 0.05% of Okaiken's Value Added Tax. KTK operates five terrestrial television services (KTK:1, KTK:2, KTK:E, KTK:City, and KTK:5), three satellite television services broadcasting in 4K HDR, and three national radio networks. KTK also provides an international broadcasting service, KTK:World.

KTK's earliest forerunner was the Okami Broadcasting Station founded in 1924. The organization was split into several companies following the Great Adonian War, with RTK (focusing on men-oriented shows, news, and action programming) introducing the popular [[Taisen drama]]s in 1961, while RMT (focusing on educational shows and women-oriented programming) started the equally popular [[Onnamono drama]]s in 1957. Both merged with Radio Okaiken in 1964 to create the modern KTK.

===Taisen drama===
{{Main|Taisen drama}}
[[File:Taisen_Drama_Opening.jpg|thumb|right|250px|The opening title sequence of a Taisen drama on KTK:1.]]

{{nihongo|'''Taisen drama'''|大戦ドラマ|"Big Battle Drama"}} is the name KTK gives to the annual, year-long historical fiction television series it broadcasts in Okaiken. Beginning in 1963 with the black-and-white ''Hana no Shōgai'', the 45-minute show airs on KTK:1 every Sunday at 20:00. The main focus of these dramas is warfare, hence the name, though they also focus on important persons and events in Sanese history.

The concept was introduced in the late 1950s as a response to the withdrawal of occupation forces. Sword ownership bans were lifted in 1956 and several castles destroyed in the Great War were rebuilt, sparking a cultural revival of interest in the samurai and ''bushidō''. RTK, struggling to attract viewers from RMT's Onnamono Dramas, created a series of shorts called ''Samurai Monogatari'', which eventually evolved into the Taisen drama format.

The 1980s are often cited as the "Golden Age" of the Taisen drama, with the 1983 series ''Honda Shigetomo'' achieving an unprecedented 41.6% average viewing audience. The most significant modern milestone occurred in 2014 with ''Honda Tennō'', which utilized 4K resolution for the first time and surpassed all previous viewership records with a staggering 44.9% average. Series 51, ''ABBASID'' (2012), was the first to be set entirely outside of Okaiken, while the 60th series, ''Oni-Motonobu'' (2021), revitalized interest in the mid-Sengoku period by focusing on the visceral physicality of the "Demon Admiral." The current series, ''Hatsu: Shiokaze no Kōgō'' (2026), follows the daughter of Anjō Motonobu and primary wife of Honda Shigetada.

===Onnamono drama===
The counterpart to the Taisen drama, {{nihongo|'''Onnamono drama'''|女の物|"Women's Tales"}} portrays women as the main character. Started by RMT in 1957, these dramas focus on the domestic, emotional, and social struggles of women across different periods of Sanese history, and have maintained a loyal viewership that rivals the Taisen drama's male-dominated audience.

==Customs and traditions==
===National holidays===
{{Main|National Holidays of Okaiken}}
Public holidays in Okaiken were established by the ''Public Holiday Law'' of 1953, with significant amendments following the Jutsu Restoration of 2011 to reflect the return of the Monarchy. The modern calendar is a unique hybrid that attempts to reconcile the nation's fractured history, balancing the secular traditions of the Republic Era with revived Imperial rites. The most notable cluster of holidays is the {{nihongo|Grand Week|大週間|Dai-shūkan}} in early June, a string of five consecutive holidays (June 3–7) that serves as the primary vacation period for the Sanese workforce.

The calendar also reflects the unique inverted geography of the archipelago. {{nihongo|Day of the Frost|霜の日|Shimo no hi}} on December 23 marks the peak of winter and is particularly celebrated in the southern island of [[Yagikoshu]], where the sub-arctic climate produces heavy snowfall, in stark contrast to the subtropical warmth of the northern provinces. When typhoons force communities inside during late summer holidays, children and adults rapidly adapt their play to makeshift indoor landscapes—a culture of {{nihongo|"ludic resilience"|遊戯的強靭性|Yūgiteki kyōjinsei}} that turns severe weather disruption into a celebration of community adaptability.

Every four Adonian years, on the intercalary February 31, Okaiken observes the {{nihongo|Day of the Dragon|龍の日|Ryū no hi}}—a major national holiday tied to the inferior conjunction of the gas giant [[Quetzal]] with [[Adonia]]. Celebrations include coastal watch parties for the daytime transit, massive bonfires, communal feasts of spicy red oden and mārā gyūbara nabe, and large-scale public sports events symbolizing the nation's physical resilience against the overwhelming tidal forces.

===Social customs===
The {{nihongo|''nenja''|念者}} tradition of male physical intimacy, rooted in the Sengoku-era {{nihongo|''wakashū''|若衆}} mentorship system, remains culturally normalized in the context of gymnasium culture and communal bathing. The {{nihongo|''tesshin-yu''|鉄身湯|iron body bath}} in Iron Body Centers serves the dual function of post-training recovery and community bonding, preserving the communal bathing tradition that has characterized Sanese social life for centuries.

The {{nihongo|companion registration|伴侶登録|hanryo tōroku}} system provides a legal framework for interpersonal relationships that exists alongside traditional marriage, reflecting the pragmatic Sanese approach to social organization.

